{
  "findings": [
    {
      "id": "execute-result",
      "text": "On a freshly constructed closed breaker, Execute admits the request and increments Requests before running it. If the admission generation remains current, Execute classifies the returned error with IsSuccessful, records the outcome, and returns the original result and error, preserving any object identity they carry. A custom classifier can therefore count a non-nil error as success or a nil error as failure. The default counts nil as success. In the closed state a success resets the consecutive failure count. A failure updates the counts before ReadyToTrip receives its copy. With the default predicate and no intervening reset or success, the sixth consecutive failure opens the breaker and clears the counters before Execute returns the sixth request's result and error.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 243,
        "fragment": "cb.afterRequest(generation, cb.isSuccessful(err))"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "admission-limits",
      "text": "If the breaker is still open when admission checks the clock, Execute returns (nil, ErrOpenState) without invoking the request, and Allow returns (nil, ErrOpenState) without issuing a callback. In half-open, the first MaxRequests admissions increment Requests, and the next admission returns the shared ErrTooManyRequests value once Requests equals the limit. Neither rejection increments Requests. A configured zero limit means one. In normal use with one report per admission, MaxRequests successful reports close the breaker, and the first failed report reopens it. Either transition clears the counters.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 285,
        "fragment": "cb.counts.Requests >= cb.maxRequests"
      },
      "question": ""
    },
    {
      "id": "allow-callback-lifecycle",
      "text": "Allow increments Requests on admission, then returns a callback that captures the admission generation and reports the supplied boolean directly, bypassing IsSuccessful. In a fresh half-open generation with MaxRequests set to 2, calling Allow once and invoking that same callback with true twice closes the breaker and clears its counts even though Requests was only 1. If two requests are admitted and one callback is never invoked, one reported success leaves the breaker half-open at capacity. Further admissions return ErrTooManyRequests until the outstanding callback reports or another retained callback causes a transition. Half-open has no expiry, and Allow provides no cancellation or capacity-release operation.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 272,
        "fragment": "tscb.cb.afterRequest(generation, success)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Is exactly one invocation per successful Allow a caller obligation, and how should callers report work they abandon?"
    },
    {
      "id": "expiry-and-stale-completion",
      "text": "When an open timeout or a positive closed interval has elapsed, the clock alone does not change the stored state or counts. Counts returns a copy without checking expiry. The next State call, admission attempt, or outcome report samples time under the lock. If the sample is strictly after expiry, an open breaker becomes half-open, or a closed breaker clears its counts and starts a new interval from that sample. An outcome report performs this refresh before checking its admission generation, so a stale report can trigger an expired open breaker's state-change callback but cannot count its own outcome. Already admitted Execute work can still run and return its original result after another call changes the generation. A zero or negative interval disables periodic closed-state clearing, and a zero or negative timeout defaults to 60 seconds.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 298,
        "fragment": "state, generation := cb.currentState(now)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the Timeout documentation explicitly say that the half-open transition waits for an operation to observe expiry?"
    },
    {
      "id": "synchronous-hooks",
      "text": "When a call triggers a state transition, it changes the state and resets the generation, counts, and expiry before invoking OnStateChange synchronously under the breaker mutex. The triggering call cannot return until the hook returns. If that hook calls State or Counts on the same breaker, it deadlocks on the mutex. ReadyToTrip likewise runs under the mutex after recording a closed-state failure and before any resulting transition. IsSuccessful runs outside the mutex after the request returns. Name returns the configured string without locking, and the two-step accessors delegate to the underlying breaker.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 359,
        "fragment": "cb.onStateChange(cb.name, prev, state)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should the callback contract document synchronous execution and which breaker methods can be called from each hook?"
    },
    {
      "id": "execute-panic-scope",
      "text": "For an admitted Execute, a request panic recovered with a non-nil value causes a failure report before Execute re-panics with the recovered value, provided the report returns normally. The report is still subject to the generation check. The same deferred handler also catches panics after the request returns. For example, with an unchanged closed generation and a ReadyToTrip hook that panics on its first invocation and returns false on its second, a request returning an error first increments the failure counts and panics in the hook. Execute then reports a second failure for that one admission and re-panics with the hook's first panic value. If the second report itself panics, the explicit re-panic is never reached.",
      "citation": {
        "line": 237,
        "fragment": "cb.afterRequest(generation, false)"
      },
      "question": "OPEN: Should panic-to-failure handling cover classifier and bookkeeping hooks, and should a hook panic be able to count a single admission twice?"
    }
  ]
}
